concept:fair-work-act-2009-sec-246 a cml:Concept ;
    skos:prefLabel "246 FWC’s assistance"@en ;
    cml:realizedBy doc:sec-246 ;
    values:originalText "Application of this section (1) This section applies if a supported bargaining authorisation is in operation in relation to a proposed multi-enterprise agreement. FWC’s assistance (2) The FWC may, on its own initiative, provide to the bargaining representatives for the agreement such assistance: (a) that the FWC considers appropriate to facilitate bargaining for the agreement; and (b) that the FWC could provide if it were dealing with a dispute. Note: This section does not empower the FWC to arbitrate, because subsection 595(3) provides that the FWC may arbitrate only if expressly authorised to do so. FWC may direct a person to attend a conference (3) Without limiting subsection (2), the FWC may provide assistance by directing a person who is not an employer specified in the authorisation to attend a conference at a specified time and place if the FWC is satisfied that the person exercises such a degree of control over the terms and conditions of the employees who will be  Chapter 2  Terms and conditions of employment" ;
    skos:definition "Application of this section (1) This section applies if a supported bargaining authorisation is in operation in relation to a proposed multi-enterprise agreement. FWC’s assistance (2) The FWC may, on its own initiative, provide to the bargaining representatives for the agreement such assistance: (a) that the FWC considers appropriate to facilitate bargaining for the agreement; and (b) that the FWC could provide if it were dealing with a dispute. Note: This section does not empower the FWC to arbitrate, because subsection 595(3) provides that the FWC may arbitrate only if expressly authorised to do so. FWC may direct a person to attend a conference (3) Without limiting subsection (2), the FWC may provide assistance by directing a person who is not an employer specified in the authorisation to attend a conference at a specified time and place if the FWC is satisfied that the person exercises such a degree of control over the terms and conditions of the employees who will be  Chapter 2  Terms and conditions of employment"@en ;
    cml:integrityHash "0ccdf42586937399ceba0f448d92994a11335c8f0a2c4f649309a780bb974a92" ;
    cof:pageNumber "489"^^xsd:integer ;
    cml:hasPart concept:fair-work-act-2009-sec-246-ss-1 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:fair-work-act-2009-sec-246-ss-2 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:fair-work-act-2009-sec-246-ss-3 ;
    cml:curationStatus cml:Proposed ;
    cml:schemaVersion "2" ;
    cml:proposedBy <https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml> ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <fair-work-act-2009.pdf> ;
    dc:source "fair-work-act-2009.pdf, p.489" .
doc:sec-246 a cof:Section ;
    cof:title "246 FWC’s assistance" ;
    values:kind "section" ;
    values:originalText "Application of this section (1) This section applies if a supported bargaining authorisation is in operation in relation to a proposed multi-enterprise agreement. FWC’s assistance (2) The FWC may, on its own initiative, provide to the bargaining representatives for the agreement such assistance: (a) that the FWC considers appropriate to facilitate bargaining for the agreement; and (b) that the FWC could provide if it were dealing with a dispute. Note: This section does not empower the FWC to arbitrate, because subsection 595(3) provides that the FWC may arbitrate only if expressly authorised to do so. FWC may direct a person to attend a conference (3) Without limiting subsection (2), the FWC may provide assistance by directing a person who is not an employer specified in the authorisation to attend a conference at a specified time and place if the FWC is satisfied that the person exercises such a degree of control over the terms and conditions of the employees who will be  Chapter 2  Terms and conditions of employment" ;
    cof:pageNumber "489"^^xsd:integer .

